    Dual-export shim too.
  - [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m (changed)[0m — inputs gain [38;5;153mname[39m attributes (they currently have only [38;5;153mid[39ms, and [38;5;153mFormData[39m keys off [38;5;153mname[39m), and
    two [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags are added before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[39m (changed)[0m — [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m is deleted; the submit handler becomes read values → validate against a schema →
    show errors or call [38;5;153mlogin[39m. The [38;5;153mlogin[39m stub and [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m are untouched.

  One deliberate boundary: the core never learns where errors get rendered, and the display helper never learns what
  makes a value invalid. That's what keeps the core testable in plain Node.

  On field-to-error matching, the display helper looks inside the form for [38;5;153m[data-error-for="<fieldname>"][39m and writes the
  message there. If no such element exists it creates one after the input, so a new form works without ceremonial
  markup but can override placement. It also sets [38;5;153maria-invalid[39m and wires [38;5;153maria-describedby[39m, since error text that only
  sighted users can associate with a field is a bug worth not shipping.

  Two decisions I need from you on this section. On the second one, the fork is whether the abstraction gets a real
  second consumer now: with one caller, "reusable" is an untested claim — the usual way a shared module ends up shaped
  wrong is that it only ever served one form. Adding a signup form to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (email, password, confirm-less, age)
  exercises [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, and [38;5;153mmin[39m and proves the schema approach holds. The cost is scope: it's a new form you
  didn't ask for, and it's throwaway if you have real forms coming. My recommendation is to add it, unless you already
  know what the next real form is.
